--- A function that answers nothing but can fail, whose body ENDS in an `if` or a `while`,
--- does not build compiled.
---
--- Interpreted (crow run -i): prints
---     big
---     small
--- Compiled (crow run): the program does not build:
---     error[E0308]: mismatched types
---       --> src/main.rs:31:20
---     error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler refused what Crow
---     accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program (`crow run -i` runs it meanwhile)
--- Expected: the same output as interpreted.
---
--- `check` can fail (its condition decides on `int[s]`), so it is emitted as
--- `fn check(s: &String) -> CrowResult<()>`; a body ending in a plain statement is
--- wrapped (`return CrowResult { ok: true, value: log(…), … }`), but one ending in an
--- `if`/`else`, an `if` with no `else`, or a `while` ends on that statement, of type `()`.
--- The same failure with an `if` that decides on a plain int, as long as the function can
--- fail somewhere above it. Related to findings/15_puzzle_game-1.co (a bare `return` in
--- such a function is printed `return;`). Found writing rosetta/15_puzzle_game.co, whose
--- game loop ends `if board eq goal … else …`.

fn check[s]
    n = int[s]
    if n gt 5
        log['big']
    else
        log['small']

check['7']
check['2']
